  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.5. Increased capacity of constituents to develop fair and effective labour migration frameworks, institutions and services to protect migrant workers</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Given the devastating impact of the pandemic on migrant workers in SADC countries in particular, the ILO piloted an income relief activity to benefit migrant domestic workers in Botswana, Lesotho and South Africa. In the case of Lesotho, a successful activity was completed to support migrant domestic workers in collaboration with the Lesotho Trade Union Congress (LTUC). As part of this activity, both protective equipment and food parcels have been made available to these workers.  In total 186 domestic workers were reached in three districts in Lesotho (Berea, Leribe and Mohale&#x2019;s Hoek). Delivery of PPE and food parcels was organised in collaboration with local authorities (in particular Chiefs). The LTUC reportedly already gained new members due to the high visibility of the activities. With assistance from the European Commission, ILO supported the provision of facemasks, sanitizers and food parcels was designed and planned in collaboration with the Lesotho Trade Union Congress, and in accordance with similar activities undertaken by ILO in Southern Africa. Apart from the design of the activity, regular meetings with the LTUC were held to discuss implementation of the activities.  Activities were captured via photos and videos.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">7.5.2. Number of countries with new or improved services to protect the labour rights of migrant workers</narrative>
    </title>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2021-12-31"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
